 go too hard and too fast and you  burn out  you're never going to improve your  health long term  and research suggests that consistency  is really the key with any health  promoting habit  so i do recommend experimenting to find  something that you actually enjoy  and trying to incorporate it at a pace  that feels sustainable to you  i want to talk about substitutes i  personally  don't buy anything that's labeled low  fat lights  diet the chemicals that they have added  into that food  so that it still tastes good without the  sugar or without the fat  are doing you so much more harm than the  sugar or the fat  okay so lots to say about this  so for the most part i am in agreement  that  when you swap out a food that you love  for a diet version  you often end up eating around your  craving  on what i like to call the satisfaction  hunt more time than not  this results in overeating i mean i talk  about it all the time  you want the oreo so you eat the fruit  salad then the rice cakes and the  fat-free  pudding then the granola bar and then  you binge on a whole box  of oreos but had you just allowed  yourself a mindful portion of the cookie  that you really craved  you probably would end up taking in a  lot fewer calories  and could just kind of move on with the  rest of your life  without investing so much time and  mental energy  on the hunt now specific to hannah's  discussion on  light aka low fat or low sugar versions  of regular foods  it is true that most of the time food  manufacturers have to replace a  desirable substance that they're taking  out  with something else so sometimes this  makes the food  actually less satisfying because it just  doesn't taste as good and  other times it also makes it less  physically satiating as well so consider  for a second  light or lower fat peanut butter as an  example  when we take out some of that fat we  need to replace it  with sugar and or salt the calories  actually are hardly impacted  but now we've got a spread that doesn't  even keep us  as full for as long and also doesn't  taste as good  so in those cases i definitely say  go with the real deal or at least go  with  whatever feels more delicious and  satisfying to you  but it's also worth just flagging that  hannah's rationale for this is not so  much about the satisfaction  or satiety factors but that there are  apparently  chemicals added into these diet foods  that are  far worse than actual sugar or fat  so yeah let's  unpack  [Music]  now i get that by chemicals hannah  probably means  additives and preservatives which is not  untrue  so a lot of lower fat products need  extra  stabilizers or gums added to them to  give them the same stability  and mouth feel that we would enjoy from  actual fat  but it's important to note that the word  chemicals  sounds way scarier than it actually is  and food ingredient fear-mongering is  literally one of my biggest  pet peeves of life folks  everything we eat is made out of  chemicals  so for example did you know that the  chemical  cold calciferol is found lurking in a  wide range of packaged processed foods  sounds pretty scary right well this  chemical is actually just vitamin d3  and yeah that fortification is helping a  lot of us  get our daily fix without the risk of  skin cancer from  excess sun exposure my point here is  just to say that demonizing the word  chemicals  is counterproductive to helping people  feel at ease  with making less diety food choices in  their day  this is what happens when we trade diet  culture with wellness culture  which is arguably even more pervasive  and problematic  but anyways that is a ted talk for  another day  let's see what else she has to say let  me tell you about my kind of substitutes  lentil pasta pea pasta chickpea pasta  rye bread instead of white bread dark  chocolate  they're my kind of substitutes the  nutritional value and the micronutrients  in those substitutes  are far better than in the kind of more  processed alternatives  okay so hannah says she likes to replace  some of her favorite food choices  with more nutrient-dense swaps like  treating  in a bean based pasta for white pasta  whole grain bread for white bread  and a dark chocolate for milk chocolate  and these choices  sound great to me as long as they are  enjoyable to her  but how healthy these healthy swaps  are ultimately comes down to intention  to me  if you're choosing dark chocolate  because it feels good to you  amazing you go get that dark chocolate  queen  i say enjoy the physical and emotional  satisfaction  that you get from making that choice and  in hannah's case  it does sound like she still enjoys a  good dark chocolate  or bean based pasta and is also  motivated by the added nutrition in  these which i think is great  i also choose chickpea pasta all the  time because  i love the added fiber and protein on  board to really keep me  and my kiddos satisfied and to me  it's just a really easy swap to make  most of the time  that doesn't trigger scarcity mentality  to me  but if you're choosing it out of food  fear for the  fake ingredients or chemicals in like a  dairy milk bar  then i do think that you risk diet  rebellion and  potentially a binge later on take more  time  to try and learn new recipes eating  home-cooked food  is always going to be the best option if  you're looking to manage your weight in  some way when you eat out  that you have no idea what they've added  into that food so unless you're ordering  from somewhere that's like an organic  it really is better to make your own  food at home learning how to cook  wholesome nutritious food is honestly so  key okay so obviously i'm a big believer  in home cooking so  i do love this tip i also love that  hannah is trying to encourage folks who  maybe don't have the best track record  or history with cooking  that it's just never too late to learn  amen to that and yes research does show  that  restaurant food tends to be higher in  calories fat saturated fat and salt  when compared with home-cooked food  there's really no  denying that but i think it's really  important that we don't stress ourselves  out about this  and let it interfere with our enjoyment  of special social experiences  fearing food that you don't prepare  yourself is one of the big  red flags of orthorexia so i do think  that improving our relationship with  food  requires tolerating some uncertainty  around what is in our food or how it's  being prepared  but i do want to comment on her  suggestion that this isn't really a  concern  as long as the restaurant is an organic  whole foods place  um you do know that organic restaurants  use a lot of sugar salt and fat too  right  and that organic maple syrup has just as  many calories  and as much sugar as non-organic white  sugar right  organic also doesn't necessarily mean  healthier  i mean this is a big conversation and i  have touched on it before in my  sorella more video right here but unless  your concern is specifically  things like genetically modified oils  there's  probably not a huge difference in the  ingredients  used or the nutrition content of the  meals  from an organic versus a non-organic  restaurant  so yes i don't disagree that we should  aim to cook the majority of our meals  ourselves at home  especially if calories are a concern  because we know that like  cooking at home allows us to customize  our meals to our diet needs  to focus on more appropriate portions  for us to limit  waste and to save money which are  all valid things in my books but my take  home here is that  organics shouldn't get like a free pass  and when you do go out to enjoy a meal  enjoy it enjoy the food enjoy the  experience  stressing over what's hidden in the food  that is  bad for your health is what is actually  bad for your health  please don't underestimate how important  shifting your  mindset and your mental well-being is  i've said this before in another video  but you cannot hate  yourself healthy okay so i love the  quote you cannot  hate yourself healthy so remember what i  said earlier  health and wellness is all about the  long game  it's really about figuring out which  health promoting behaviors  you can do for the rest of your life  without making yourself  unhappy and science really does echo  this sentiment  so research has found that  non-stigmatizing more supportive  techniques  are preferred and are more successful  for promoting healthy behaviors  over a typical tough love approach  so yeah i say give yourself  some grace and patience is please listen  to your body  you shouldn't try to outsmart trick or  ignore your body  if you're hungry please eat that is your  body telling you that you need  fuel if every single time you eat a meal  you're getting like really  painful bloating rather than punishing  yourself please see an expert  and get their opinion okay so this is  really good advice that i definitely  agree with  and you know i say it a lot listen  to your body but i will say that as i  grow in this  body neutrality intuitive eating space i  also want to acknowledge that  i know it can be really overly  simplistic as well  and also probably not very helpful for a  lot of folks  who've been trapped in diet culture and  restriction for so long  i also appreciate that a lot of people  may just need more guidance and more  structure  and saying just listen to your body  might feel  really stressful or even impossible for  some folks  some people may not know what feels good  to their body  after just like listening to external  cues for just so long in their life  so if you're just getting started on  listening to your body  but maybe you don't feel hunger or you  don't feel hunger until it's like an  intense  give me all the food experience consider  eating  like a small meal every few hours to  just encourage those cues to return  and i also agree that if you're  experiencing digestive symptoms  after every meal or snack please  definitely speak to a doctor before  like self-diagnosing or cutting out a  bunch of foods  this often can lead to food fears  and also can potentially create  digestive symptoms  thanks to the intense interaction  between our gut and brain  and if you hop right into eliminations  without speaking to a professional  first it also can make it more difficult  to rule out  diseases like celiac for example since  you need to actually be eating things  like gluten to properly  test for the condition anyways i am glad  to hear that hannah has made some  changes to help improve her relationship  with her body  and herself and food and to start to  shed some  of her diet culture beliefs of her past  but before i close off  i do want to quickly touch on some other  comments and messages that i came across  while perusing her youtube and social  media channels  now according to an instagram q a on her  page hannah went fully plant-based in  january 2020 for ethical reasons at  first  but she soon found that it also made it  easier  for her to maintain her weight loss  because she likes to eat  big portions and this does make sense  seeing as plant-based foods are  naturally typically  higher in fiber and usually less  calorically dense  compared to animal based products so for  example  putting together a 400 calorie salad of  veggies and chickpeas  is going to yield a significantly larger  portion than  400 calories of steak with cheese but  ultimately this comes down to satiety  and satisfaction  on a caloric deficit i could think of a  lot of calorically dense foods that are  also vegan  like fries are vegan candies vegan there  are vegan cookies and burgers and ice  creams and cakes and pizza etc  so vegan doesn't necessarily mean locale  or volume dense and on the flip side  being an omnivore doesn't mean you can't  enjoy  big portions of salad or fruit or high  fiber whole grains or beans  or other higher volume lower calorie  foods  again the key is sustainability  and if giving yourself a rule that you  can't eat your beloved cheese  triggers a scarcity mentality that leads  you to  plow through a pint of oat milk ice  cream every single night  you're probably not going to lose weight  but if you  naturally enjoy choosing those higher  volume higher fiber  plant-based foods then of course it's  going to be easier for you to sustain  and maintain and therefore potentially  to lose weight  and keep it off now i also watched a  quick what in a day video from hannah's  channel  just to get a sense of how her advice  plays out in her daily diet in real life  and to summarize hannah's day of eating  is  very similar to what she describes so  she has a vegan bacon sandwich for  breakfast  avocado toast for lunch and a portion of  chickpea pasta with veggies for dinner  she also does a little haul of all of  her favorite vegan snack foods which  all look totally delicious so it is  refreshing to see someone eat  like a whole foods plant-based diet with  some more convenience foods  mixed in which i just find feels more  sustainable  and accessible to most people watching  from home  and while i did find her calories and  protein intake was maybe a little bit  lower than where  i would personally want them i get that  she is on a weight management journey  and as such  she might just be used to eating in a  more significant deficit  so in conclusion i do agree with a lot  of hannah's sentiments in her viral  weight loss video particularly the  take-home message  to really appreciate and listen to your  body and practice  self-love i do think it's worth doing a  little bit more soul-searching on her  sentiments around chemicals  and so the more moralizing language that  i heard here  and also maybe a little research herself  on the experts that she chooses to trust  but i mean we're all learning what feels  best to our body  so it's possible that with a little bit  more experimentation and  data collection that she may just find a  place that  better supports her mental and physical  health  and ultimately i do believe that her  sentiments are coming from a really good  place  and that her channel feels relatively  benign in the context of  the pseudoscience wellness youtube  community  so i do wish her lots of luck and  support on the rest of her journey  and on that note that is all that i have  for you guys today folks thank you again  to cerebral for sponsoring this video if  you liked it be sure to give it that  thumbs up  leave me a comment below on who you'd  like seeing your view next  subscribe to the channel and i will see  you next time on abby's kitchen  bye and for more great videos like this  check out the links right here
 

